This expression is now skolemized:

$$
\mathrm {F} (\mathrm {b}) \wedge \mathrm {M} (\mathrm {c}, \mathrm {b}) \wedge \mathrm {G} (\mathrm {b}))
$$

This can be expressed as the following set of clauses:

$$
\{\{ \mathrm {F} (\mathrm {b}) \}, \{\mathrm {M} (\mathrm {c}, \mathrm {b}) \}, \{\mathrm {G} (\mathrm {b}) \} \}
$$

Now we have arrived at a set of clauses, upon which resolution can be applied. The clauses we have are the following:

1. {C(a)}

2. { $\neg F ( y )$ , L( a, y ) }

3. {¬C(x), ¬F(y), ¬(G(y), ¬L(x,y))}

4. { $\neg\mathrm{F}(\mathrm{x}), \neg\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{c},\mathrm{x}), \neg\mathrm{C}(\mathrm{y}), \mathrm{L}(\mathrm{y},\mathrm{x})$ }

5. {F(b)}

6. {M(c,b)}

7. {G(b)}

We now apply resolution as follows:

First, we unify lines 1 and 3 using $\{a/x\}$ and resolve these two, to give

$$
8. \quad \{\neg F (y), \neg (G (y), \neg L (a, y)) \}
$$

Similarly, the unifier $\{b / y\}$ can be applied to lines 2 and 5, which are then resolved to give

9. {L(a,b)}

Now we apply $\{b / y\}$ to resolve line 5 with line 8 to give

10. {¬(G(b), ¬L(a,b)}

Now lines 9 and 10 can be resolved to give

11. {¬(G(b))}

Finally, line 7 can be resolved with line 11 to give

12. $\bot$

Hence, we have proven that the set of clauses derived from the premises 1, 2,and 3,and the negation of the conclusion, are unsatisfiable. Thus we have successfully proved that the conclusion does indeed follow from the premises, and so the argument is a valid one.
